    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d421">

                <pb facs="0403" n="395" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg395"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.11/2553</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Berle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">of a
                        Conversation With the Japanese Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Horinouchi</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-07-09">July 9, 1940</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Japanese Ambassador called today, at his request.</p>
                <p>He brought up the interview given by Mr. Early to the newspapers. He said this
                    somewhat confused him, because it was thought that there was a discrepancy
                    between the President’s statement and the statement of the Secretary of State on
                    the Monroe Doctrine.</p>
                <p>I said that there was no discrepancy in fact, though I did not undertake to say
                    that some of the newspaper commentators might not have erected one. On the
                    contrary, the President had taken occasion to say that he stood wholeheartedly
                    behind the Secretary’s point of view.</p>
                <p>I said that Secretary Early’s statements, taken out of their context, might
                    create the impression of discrepancy, but that there was none, in fact.</p>
                <closer><signed>A. A. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berle, Jr</hi>.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
